Team,

Annex distilling Calthorpe Part 6 onto a single page. Use this as the
working anchor for the competitor-positioning slides in the target
assessment and any competitor depiction in the market overview map.
All figures triangulated from Calthorpe Part 6 plus the Hartley Pearce
NZ Egg Producers Competitor Overview (hp-agri-nz-027), the Navis
Capital - Mainland Poultry 2017 press release, MainFeeds public
materials, and Cal-Maine Foods public news releases (Echo Lake,
Tyson facilities, Deal-Rite Feeds).
The tables are attached as screenshots of my working annex - happy
to send the underlying file if anyone wants to rework the numbers.

- Iris

1. vertical-integration depth per producer

Anchor: Mainland is the only NZ shell-egg producer with a
full seven-stage stack. Every other producer is mid-stack
or narrower.

key point (Calthorpe Part 6 [00:09:48]): "Aurora's #2 share by
volume sits alongside #4 or #5 ranking on integration depth.
That asymmetry matters when you sit a 12-14x EBITDA broker
asking next to the Mainland-anchored 7.0-7.5x PEP-2025
precedent. Mainland traded at 7.0-7.5x with the full stack.
Aurora without the full stack does not earn a multiple uplift
over that comp - it earns a structural discount."

Mainland full-stack anchors (verifiable real public sources):
  - 1.2 million layer birds across 7 farms (Navis 2017 press
    release).
  - 17 sites total, 635 ha sensitive land (OIO public records).
  - MainFeeds: 3 mills (Otago / Auckland / Manawatu); approx
    140,000 tonnes compound feed annually (MainFeeds public
    materials).
  - Owns Farmer Brown and Woodland retail brands (Navis 2017).
  - Holds category-captain status with Foodstuffs and
    Woolworths NZ (Calthorpe Part 4 + Part 6).

2. per-producer revenue / EBITDA margin / brand-vs-pl

FY24 working anchors (Hartley Pearce + Calthorpe Part 6
triangulation; privately-held caveat applies):

notes:
- Aurora's 50/50 brand-vs-PL is the most PL-exposed share of
  the top-five excluding Zeagold's industrial book - middle-
  of-pack EBITDA margin and worst PL exposure together rank
  Aurora structurally below its #2 volume share (Calthorpe
  Part 6 [00:15:48]).
- Zeagold's 30:70 split is mostly industrial / egg-products,
  not retail PL; their margin holds up through the
  egg-products mix.
- Henergy's 75:25 brand-skew is the highest of the cohort,
  driven by their pure-play free-range positioning.
- Mainland's 70:30 is protected by Farmer Brown + Woodland
  brand portfolio and the category-captain planogram lever.

verbatim Calthorpe Part 6 [00:15:48]: "Aurora at NZ$84.6M FY24
revenue and 12.8% EBITDA margin sits in the cohort but on the
wrong side of two of the three operating dimensions. Their
EBITDA margin is below the Mainland 14-16% range and middle-
of-pack overall. Their brand-vs-PL split is approximately
50/50 - which is the most PL-exposed share of the top-five
excluding Zeagold's industrial book. So Aurora at #2 by share
is actually mid-pack to bottom-tier on margin and PL exposure."

3. geographic concentration

Aurora's two-site footprint is materially thinner than
Mainland's seven-farm / 17-site / three-feed-mill network.
Single-site failure (biosecurity event, fire, regulatory
shutdown) is a step-change higher exposure. Worth flagging
in the risk register as concentration risk that does not
show up in customer-concentration metrics (Calthorpe Part 6
[00:21:48]).

H7N6 Otago 2024 was localized. Mainland's dual-island spread
contained the disruption; a single-island producer would have
faced more material disruption. Aurora's dual-island spread
is partial protection but with only one site per island the
single-site-failure exposure remains.

4. niche specialty positions

Better Eggs has the deepest specialty bench despite being
only #4 by volume share (9%) - they range omega-3, pasture-
raised (via Smith-family Hawke's Bay 2023 acquisition), and
certified organic. Disproportionately important on the
premium-tier consumer-demand picture (covered in Part 5).

Henergy is pure-play free-range but does not have a true
premium-tier specialty SKU above their core free-range; they
differentiate on welfare positioning rather than tier ladder.

Aurora has no meaningful pasture-raised, no omega-3 enriched,
no certified organic SKU. That underweights the durable
specialty-premium tier (Calthorpe Part 5 + Part 6).

long-tail specialty cohort (Hartley Pearce roll-up framework):
5-8 named operators (Hens-on-Pasture, Tasman Egg Co., two
Otago specialists, three Waikato boutique operators), total
flock approximately 150-200k birds combined, 5-6x EBITDA
multiples on NZ$3-8M each, total NZ$30-50M to roll up the
specialty book. Available post-acquisition top-line lever for
Aurora's owner; not a defensive move because Aurora is not
currently in the tier.

co-operative / collective producer angle (Calthorpe Part 6
[00:27:14] + [00:47:48]): A couple of smaller commercial
operators are exploring purchasing-and-distribution co-ops to
retain Pams / Pams Finest tender access through Phase 2. If
those co-ops form they may become small-but-real new entrants
at the bottom of the share roster. Pams tenders structurally
go to one or two suppliers per category, so a co-op with
enough scale to hold a tender is a real new competitor, not
a mom-and-pop story.

5. capital availability through phase 2 (rationalization lever)

Phase 2 conversion capex (Calthorpe Part 2): NZ$45-65 per
bird. Implied dollar requirement per producer:

Capital availability is the single biggest structural
rationalization lever. By FY28 Calthorpe expects 6-10
long-tail operators to exit via sale or wind-down. That
roll-up opportunity is post-acquisition optionality for an
institutionally-funded Aurora owner, not a baseline thesis
input.

Foreign Capital Appetite For NZ Agri - established. Real
named precedents: Navis Capital (2017 Mainland), Pacific
Equity Partners (2025 Mainland take-out from Navis), Direct
Capital, Macquarie Infrastructure Real Assets, Adamantem
Capital - all AU/NZ mid-market sponsors who have done NZ agri
at this scale tier. US mid-market sponsors are rarer because
absolute deal size sits below most US institutional minimums;
Halberd's trans-Pacific tuck-in framing fits an under-served
gap but does not remove the scale-haircut question
(Calthorpe Part 6 [00:33:48]).

