Cal-Maine comp commentary (Calthorpe Part 6 [00:36:24]):
Three Cal-Maine deals in 14 months (Echo Lake, Tyson
shell-egg facilities, Deal-Rite Feeds). Two themes:
industrial egg-products adjacency, and vertical-integration
deepening into feed. Both dimensions Aurora doesn't have.
Cal-Maine's M&A pattern is consistent with the structural
read: integration deepening and processed-egg adjacency are
where premium multiples sit.

comp-set construction (Calthorpe Part 6 [00:42:48]): The
deck should build the comp set bottom-up from in-NZ-eggs
control transactions (PEP-Mainland 7.0-7.5x platform anchor,
Navis-Mainland 6.5x prior-cycle anchor, Better Eggs / Smith-
family 5-6x sub-scale anchor) with Cal-Maine 9-11x and
Inghams 8-9x as cross-trade scale-haircut comps. Cal-Maine
Echo Lake adjacency anchors the processed-egg sensitivity
only. A buyer who builds the comp set top-down from
Cal-Maine 9-11x without the scale haircut and without
engaging with PEP-Mainland 7.0-7.5x as the more direct
in-NZ precedent has not done the comp work properly.

7. summary reads for the deck

verbatim Calthorpe Part 6 [00:45:18]: "Two summary calls
for the deck. Call one - the broker outreach frames Aurora
as one of the cleaner operators with long-standing retail
relationships and no material customer churn. The honest
competitor read is - Aurora is a four-stage mid-stack
producer at #2 by volume share but #4 or #5 by integration
depth, with the most private-label-exposed mix in the top-
five excluding Zeagold's industrial book, and middle-of-
pack EBITDA margin. None of that is hidden - it's all
derivable from the Hartley Pearce competitor overview, the
Navis 2017 press release and the value-chain Part 4
conversation. The broker framing is not a lie; it's a
selective view of the volume-share dimension only. The
deck should report Aurora's structural competitive
position accurately rather than echo the broker pitch."

strategic-environment flags (Calthorpe Part 6 [00:50:18]):
  - Heyden Farms strategic process potentially in next
    12-18 months (Q4 2025 industry commentary). Could
    consolidate the South Island competitive footprint
    (Aurora's South Island site sits in the same area as
    Heyden's Christchurch base).
  - Mainland under PEP will be harder, not softer, on
    competitive pricing through Phase 2. PEP's stated
    thesis: 'accelerate Phase 2 conversion and broaden
    adjacencies'. Aurora's thesis cannot assume a passive
    #1 competitor.

8. producer ownership / control structure landscape

