<table style="border-collapse:collapse;margin:12px 0;font-size:14px" border="1" cellpadding="6"><thead><tr><th>Producer</th><th>Ownership</th><th>Governance read</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mainland</td><td>Pacific Equity Partners (PEP; acquired 2025 from Navis; prior PE owner Navis 2017-2025)</td><td>Institutional board; clean decision rights; no family overhang</td></tr><tr><td>Heyden</td><td>Heyden family (2nd gen); signals of strategic process in the next 12-18 months</td><td>Family-controlled; succession overhang; decision-rights ambiguity flagged</td></tr><tr><td>Better Eggs</td><td>Anderson family (3rd gen)</td><td>Family-controlled; some external NEDs</td></tr><tr><td>Zeagold</td><td>Mainland subsidiary (PEP)</td><td>Subsumed under Mainland governance</td></tr><tr><td>Henergy</td><td>Founder-led private group</td><td>Founder-CEO; clean control</td></tr><tr><td>Aurora</td><td>McKenna family (3rd gen); considering partial sale</td><td>Family-controlled; chair-succession overhang flagged below</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Calthorpe Part 6 [00:55:48] on Aurora specifically: &#x27;I want to push back carefully on the broker&#x27;s &quot;stable family management&quot; line because it&#x27;s the standard sell-side framing on every NZ family-controlled deal and it&#x27;s almost never the full read on a transitional one. Aurora&#x27;s principal is Mark McKenna - 3rd-generation, MD - in his mid-to-late fifties, in good health, expected to continue running the business for the next decade plus. Mark himself is genuinely competent and engaged operationally. That part of the broker line is fine.</p>
<p>Where I&#x27;d push harder is on the board-side structure. Mark&#x27;s mother Eleanor McKenna is chair of the family company board. She is in her late seventies now, still actively chairing, and the industry view is that Eleanor has signalled stepping back from the chair in the next 12 to 24 months but no successor chair has been publicly named. The board is currently five seats - three family (Mark, Eleanor, and one of Eleanor&#x27;s siblings as a third family NED) and two external NEDs whose terms come up for renewal in FY26. Mark runs operations day-to-day but the chair has structural veto authority on strategic decisions - new debt facilities, major capex, transaction approvals. A partial-sale partner who assumes &quot;the McKenna family is unitary&quot; on strategic direction is going to be surprised. Eleanor and Mark have not always agreed publicly on capex pacing or on what to do with the South Island site. Plus the FY26 external-NED refresh hits in the same window as Eleanor&#x27;s likely chair transition - whoever the new capital partner is will want representation, which lands at the same time as the chair-succession question. Treat the Aurora governance / chair-succession read as a genuine confirmatory diligence item, not a tidy positive.&#x27;</p>
<p>Calthorpe Part 6 [00:58:12]: &#x27;For the market overview, the practical implication is that the producer cohort splits cleanly into PEP-controlled (Mainland + Zeagold subsumed under it), family-controlled with succession or chair overhang (Heyden, Aurora), family-controlled stable (Better Eggs), and founder-led (Henergy). That ownership-and- governance split is at least as material as the production- system mix when you depict the competitive landscape, because it shapes who can move fast on Phase 2 capex (PEP-controlled producers can; family-controlled with succession or chair overhang will be slower) and who is sale-process eligible in the next two-to-three years (Heyden almost certainly; Aurora in process now; the others not).&#x27;</p>
<p>contradiction for the deck / depiction: The broker outreach frames Aurora&#x27;s family management as &quot;stable&quot; and frames governance / continuity as a clean positive (see broker_outreach_aurora.eml). The Calthorpe read above contradicts that framing on Aurora specifically: governance is a structural confirmatory item, not a clean positive. The deck / depiction should report the producer ownership-and-governance split accurately and flag Aurora&#x27;s succession question as an open item, rather than echoing the broker line.</p>
<p>END annex</p>
